  "text": "Emergency- frederick Vet Services | Partner Veterinary\nSkip to content\nFrederick 301.200.8185\nRichmond 804.206.9122\nEmergency\nFrederick, MD\nRichmond, VA\nSpecialties\nFrederick, MD\nInternal Medicine – Frederick\nOncology – Frederick\nNeurology-Frederick\nSurgery – Frederick\nCardiology – Frederick\nRichmond, VA\nNeurology-RICHMOND\nOncology – Richmond\nCritical Care -Richmond\nCardiology – Richmond\nInternal Medicine – RICHMOND\nSurgery – Richmond\nAbout Us\nOur Doctors\nCareers\nPet Parents\nBlog\nConditions and Treatments\nPodcast\nFAQ\nKnow Before You Go\nPre-Register\nMy Pet Needs to be Hospitalized, Now What?\nPayment Options\nPet Loss\nShare Your Story\nVeterinarians\nContinuing Education\nRefer\nRequest Materials\nHome\nEmergency Frederick\nEmergency Richmond\nFrederick, MD Specialty Services\nRichmond, VA Specialty Services\nPet Parents\nVeterinarians\nOur Doctors\nCareers\nContact Us\nWe Are Here To Help!\n301-200-8185\n7330 Guilford Drive, Frederick, MD 21704\nEmergencies are never planned and can happen at any time. When care cannot wait, Partner Veterinary Emergency is available 24 hours a day, every day. With a highly-trained, compassionate team and state-of-the-art technology, you can trust that\nyour pet is in the best hands at their most critical moment.\nOur Triage Process\nOrder of treatment, or “triage,” dictates how patients are prioritized in an emergency room setting. Patients in level 1  will be treated first, followed by more stable patients. All emergency hospitals, both human and veterinary, follow this same approach to make sure all patients get the level of care they need as quickly as possible.\nLevel 1:\nResuscitation\nConditions that are threats to life or limb\nLevel 2:\nEmergent\nConditions that are a potential threat to life, limb or function\nLevel 3:\nUrgent\nSerious conditions that require emergency intervention\nLevel 4:\nLess Urgent\nConditions that relate to patient distress or potential complications that would benefit from intervention\nLevel 5:\nNon-Urgent\nConditions that are non-urgent or that may be part of a chronic problem\nCommon Emergency Symptoms\nCommon medical emergencies can include trauma injuries, ingestion dangers, and unexplained symptoms.\nHeat stroke\nNear-drowning\nBite or sting of a venomous animal, such as a snake\nCats with open mouth-breathing\nTrouble breathing/severe coughing\nTrauma (e.g., traffic accidents, falls, lacerations, head trauma, eye trauma)\nBlue, purple, or pale gums/tongue\nSepsis (severe systemic infection)\nBleeding disorders or severe bleeding\nFluid accumulation in the abdomen or chest\nAdverse chemotherapy and other drug reactions\nPossible toxin (poison) ingestion or exposure\nDifficulty urinating, urinating blood, not able to urinate\nIngestion of a foreign object\nSevere vomiting, diarrhea or unproductive retching\nSeizures or tremors\nImmune-mediated diseases\nBurns and smoke inhalation\nSudden vision compromise or loss\nAcute kidney failure\nHeart failure\nLiver failure\nDiabetic emergencies\nDeterioration of chronic conditions\nLabor complications, neonatal emergencies\nAllergic reactions – acute itching, hives, swelling\nSudden collapse, unresponsiveness\n301-200-8185\nMeet the Team\nJulie Crosby\nHospital Steward\nMiriah Wilson, DVM\nLead ER Veterinarian\nMari-Ashli Foy, DVM\nHeidi Goggin, DVM\nAmy Lauterstein, DVM\nKeri Levinson, DVM, CVA\nAdam Mauser, DVM\nGlynis McCorkle, DVM\nResidency Trained in Critical Care\nHeidi  Mostoller, DVM\nCharla F. McKoy, DVM\nContact Us\nEmergency\nFrederick Specialties\nRichmond Specialties\nCareers\nAbout Us\nContact Us\nFAQ\nVeterinarians\nOpt-out preferences\nFacebook\nInstagram\nLinkedIn\n© 2026 All Rights Reserved |\nAccessibility",
